$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General Procedure for Oxidation of Terminal Olefins. To a stirred
solution of olefin (0.5 mmol) in CH3CN (3.5 mL) and H2O (0.5 mL) were
added PdCl2 (4.4 mg, 0.025 mmol, 5 mol %) and Fe2(SO4)3·nH2O (600 mg, 1.5
mmol, 1.5 equiv) at room temperature. The reaction mixture was warmed to
45 °C and stirred for a specified time (see Tables 2 and 3) under N2. The
reaction mixture was then filtered through a small pad of silica gel and
washed with EtOAc, and the filtrate was concentrated. The residue in some
cases contained virtually pure compound, and no further purification was
necessary (for compounds 2a−c and 2f−h). In other cases the residue was
purified by silica gel column chromatography using petroleum ether/ EtOAc
as an eluent to afford the methyl ketones. Decan-2-one (2b):13i Yield
(74.2 mg, 95%). Colorless oil. 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3/TMS): δ = 2.40 (t,
J = 7.5 Hz, 2H), 2.11 (s, 3H), 1.55−1.52 (m, 2H), 1.30−1.22 (m, 10H),
0.86 (t, J = 6.9 Hz, 3H); 13C {1H} NMR (100 MHz, CDCl3): δ = 209.4, 43.8,
31.8, 29.8, 29.3, 29.14, 29.1, 23.8, 22.6, 14.1.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM optimization study (cat., oxidant, solvent, time and amt. of oxidant)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Iron(III) Sulfate as Terminal Oxidant in the Synthesis of Methyl Ketones
via Wacker Oxidation
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Fernandes, Rodney A.; Chaudhari, Dipali A.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of Organic Chemistry (2014), 79(12), 5787-5793

