$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General Pd[(-)-sparteine]Cl2 catalyzed Direct O2 coupled Wacker Oxidation
Procedure In a 75 mL schlenk flask equipped with a stirbar, 3.3 mg
Pd[(-)-sparteine]Cl2 (0.008 mmol, 0.01 equiv.) and 4 mL of a 4:1 (v/v)
solution of DMA:H2O were combined. The flask was placed in an oil bath
heated to 70 ˚C and stirred to dissolve catalyst (ca. 10 minutes), at
which time the flask was allowed to cool to room temperature. A condenser
was then attached to the flask and a three-way joint fitted with a
balloon of O2 was installed on the condenser. The flask was evacuated via
house vacuum and refilled with oxygen three times and the mixture was
stirred vigorously for ca. 10 minutes at room temperature. Using a
syringe, 178 μL of dodecene (0.8 mmol, 1 equiv.) was added at room
temperature. Following substrate addition, the reaction flask was placed
into a 70 ˚C oil bath. The reaction mixture was stirred vigorously under
a balloon of O2. After 18 h, the flask was removed from the oil bath and
condenser walls were rinsed with ca. 2 mL Et2O. The reaction mixture was
transferred to a 60 mL sep. funnel and washed twice with ca. 4 mL of 1N
HCl(aq).The aqueous layers were combined and back extracted three times
with ca. 4 mL Et2O. The organic layers were combined and washed with ca.
5 mL brine. The organic layer was dried over MgSO4 and filtered. Product
was purified from catalyst and remaining solvent via flash column
chromatography using 1-5% Et2O/pentane solvent gradient. Fractions
containing product were collected and solvent removed in vacuo. Purity
was confirmed via 1H-NMR. Table 2 Entry 1 and 2 (2-dodecanone). Purified
via flash chromatography using 1-5 % Et2O in pentane to afford
2-dodecanone in 85 % yield under O2 atm. and 71 % yield under an air atm;
confirmed via previously reported 1H-NMR.10
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM using air instead of oxygen gave lower yield
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Discovery of a Practical Direct O2-Coupled Wacker Oxidation with
Pd[(-)-sparteine]Cl2
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Cornell, Candace N.; Sigman, Matthew S.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Letters (2006), 8(18), 4117-4120

