$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: General Procedure for the Synthesis of
1,4-Diones (4). A two necked flask fitted with a septum and connected to
an oxygen filled balloon via a three-way tap was charged with PdCl2 (~
0.2 eq) and CuCl (~ 1.0 eq), treated with a solution of DMF/H2O (7:1, ~ 3
mL/mmol CuCl), and evacuated and flushed with oxygen three times. After
stirring at room temperature for 1 h, the mixture was treated via syringe
with a solution of homoallylic ketone 2 (1.0 eq) in DMF/H2O (7:1, ~ 1
mL/mmol homoallylic ketone) and stirred overnight. On occasion, if TLC
analysis revealed unreacted starting material, an additional quantity of
PdCl2 (~ 0.2 eq) was added to the mixture, and stirring was continued
until the starting material was completely consumed (TLC). The reaction
mixture was partitioned between EtOAc and HCl solution (1M aqueous) and
the layers were separated. The aqueous layer was extracted with several
portions of EtOAc, and the combined organic layers were washed with water
and brine. The aqueous washings were extracted with EtOAc, and the
combined organic phase was dried with MgSO4 and evaporated.
Lyophilization of residual DMF from the crude product afforded 1,4-dione
4 of sufficient purity for the next step.
7-(4-Methoxy-phenyl)-heptane-2,5-dione (4-A3) Prepared as a pale yellow
oil (298 mg, 93 % yield) from 2-A3 (298 mg, 1.37 mmol). 1H NMR (400 MHz,
CDCl3) δ 2.16 (s, 3H), 2.59-2.76 (m, 6H), 2.78-2.85 (m, 2H), 3.75 (s,
3H), 6.76-6.82 (m, 2H), 7.03-7.09 (m, 2H). 13C NMR (100 MHz, CDCl3) δ
28.8, 29.7, 36.2, 36.9, 44.5, 55.2, 113.9, 129.2, 133.0, 157.9, 207.1,
208.5. NMR data was consistent with the literature.7
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM combinatorial, regioselective, Tsuji-Wacker oxidation
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Three-step solution-phase combinatorial access to 1,2-disubstituted and
1,2,5-trisubstituted pyrroles from carboxylic esters
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Hansford, Karl A.; Zanzarova, Valeria; Doerr, Aurelie; Lubell, William D.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of Combinatorial Chemistry (2004), 6(6), 893-898

